{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Do not drink wine or strong drink.</b> They expounded in the Sifrei: I have only wine; how do I include the other intoxicants? Scripture says ושכר. If so, why is יין stated? For wine the punishment is death, and for the other intoxicants it is by way of warning." "<b>You and your sons with you, when you come.</b> He mentioned <b>בבואכם</b> — that is, at the time when the service of your watch is in force, for the priests were divided into twenty-four watches, and each watch served one Shabbat, and on that Shabbat alone it is forbidden to that watch to drink wine; they are permitted during the other watches on the other Shabbatot. And likewise each and every watch is permitted during all the watches except on that Shabbat alone when they serve — and this is the sense of בבואכם. And He added, saying <b>אתך</b> — 'like you': that they be fit for the priesthood. For if He had said <b>אתה ובניך</b> and had not said <b>אתך</b>, it would be implied that the warning against drinking wine applies to all the sons of Aaron; but Scripture warns only those who are fit for the priesthood, for it is possible that there are sons of Aaron, of the seed of Aaron, who are not fit for the priesthood — for example, if they married disqualified women, a divorcée or a חללה — and then their priesthood is invalidated. And this is what Scripture says (Leviticus 21:1): אמור אל הכהנים בני אהרן — it did not say 'sons of Aaron' alone, but הכהנים, who are fit for the priesthood. And He said <b>חקת עולם לדורותיכם</b> — to include under this rule all the sanctuaries: Nov, Givon, Shiloh, and Jerusalem." "<b>And since</b> Scripture said ולהבדיל ולהורות with a ו״ו, Scripture hinted to us that included under this ruling is anyone who needs to distinguish between the sacred and the profane or who needs to issue a ruling — he too is forbidden to drink wine." "<b>And know</b> that wine by its nature produces three things: sleep, haughtiness of spirit, and confusion of the intellect — and all three are alluded to in this passage. It produces sleep: this is well known, for from the dregs of wine a warm and moist vapor rises to the head, from which sleep is generated. And this is בבאכם אהל מועד, alluding to the prohibition of sleep in the Tent. This is what is written of Jacob (Genesis 28:16): וייקץ יעקב משנתו — 'and I did not know' — that is, had I known, I would not have slept nor lain down. And this is what is written (1 Samuel 3:3): ונר אלהים טרם יכבה ושמואל שוכב בהיכל ה' — its reading is 'טרם יכבה בהיכל ה׳ ושמואל שוכב במקומו,' and the reason for the disjunctive accent (etnaḥta) on the word שוכב indicates this. Also, the posture was not sitting but standing, as it says (Deuteronomy 18:7): ושרת בשם ה' אלהיו ככל אחיו הלוים העומדים, and it is written (ibid.): לעמוד לשרת בשם ה'. It produces haughtiness of spirit, for it heats the forces of the heart, and from this comes haughtiness of heart — and this is what it says: ולהבדיל בין הקדש ובין החול, for with elevation of the heart all things are equal in his eyes; he does not distinguish between sacred and profane, between impure and pure. It produces confusion of the intellect, for the vapor that rises to the brain is a separating partition between the intellect and the other forces of the body, like a cloud spread in the firmament that is a partition separating the light of the sun from this world — so too the actions of a person grow dark, and he cannot distinguish among them. And this is what it says: ולהורות את בני ישראל, for instruction in the commandments of the Torah requires a pure intellect and lucid knowledge. <br>And behold, Solomon, peace be upon him, censured excessive drinking of wine and said (Proverbs 23:31): אל תרא יין כי יתאדם. Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, explained: כי יתאדם — it craves blood (מתאוה לדם). כי יתן בכוס עינו — from the language of (Numbers 11:7) ועינו כעין הבדולח. בכוס — it is written בכיס, for when a man is drawn after the cup (כוס), he comes to lack of pocket (כיס). יתהלך במישרים — his end is to sell all the utensils of his house, and nothing remains for him, until there is no vessel left in his house in which to cook; this is יתהלך במישרים — in the end his house is empty of everything. <br>אחריתו כנחש ישך — just as the serpent enticed Eve with the taste and beauty of the fruit, as it says (Genesis 3:6): כי טוב העץ למאכל וכי תאוה הוא לעינים ונחמד העץ להשכיל, and afterward brought them to death — so too wine entices the one drawn to the beauty of its appearance and taste, and in the end brings him to death. <br>And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: just as with the serpent, the earth was cursed on its account, as it says (ibid.): ארורה האדמה בעבורך — so too with wine, a third of the world was cursed on its account, as it says (ibid. v. 9): וייקץ נח מיינו, and it is written: ארור כנען."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {3 "In the 1 Samuel 3:3 discussion, his rewording 'טרם יכבה בהיכל ה׳ ושמואל שוכב במקומו' appears to reflect a rabbinic reanalysis of the verse's accentuation; translated hyper-literally as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:48.008111049Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}